A native NSStatusItem opens an NSPopover using AnthropicSerif-Regular. It observes the existing Assets bridge through BridgeClient.mjs and never creates another archive queue. (7 October 2026)

The active skill is zenray-assets; this dashboard shows its service. (7 October 2026)

Build and install with `node scripts/Assets/NativeDashboard/Install.mjs`, then open `~/Applications/ZenRay Assets.app`. The installer validates/signs the app and installs a RunAtLoad LaunchAgent for the next login. Right-click the menu-bar label to quit only the dashboard. The Assets daemon remains running. (7 October 2026)

Settings live in the app's Contents/Resources/Settings.json: Node executable, adapter, polling intervals, font, dimensions and child timeout. Missing font and service failures are visible. Adapter pipes drain in parallel; a native watchdog sends TERM after the bounded timeout and prevents another request while that child remains alive. No SIGKILL is sent. (7 October 2026)

Only active transfers and latest failures appear; a later successful operation suppresses historical job failures. LOAD and UNLOAD use their actual service job states. Chunk preparation, confirmed uploaded bytes and verification retain different labels; no guessed speed, ETA or overall upload percentage appears. (7 October 2026)

Run unit tests with `node --test scripts/Assets/NativeDashboard/Model.test.mjs`. After installation, run `node scripts/Assets/NativeDashboard/Verify.mjs <Assets-workshop-path>` for real native fixtures covering simultaneous LOAD/UNLOAD, empty state, service failure, large stderr and child timeout. The fixtures never modify production projects. (7 October 2026)

For runtime proof, set ZENRAY_DASHBOARD_PROOF to an admitted workshop JSON path while launching the native executable with --show. It records one snapshot, font resolution, status-item presence and popover visibility plus an image of the app's own rendered content. The optional --appearance=light and --appearance=dark affect only this app. A view rendering is distinct from a computer-use screenshot. (7 October 2026)

The service still owns all authentication and cloud failures. An empty daemon secret means loopback and client-header checks, not secret authentication. Adapter credentials stay in request headers and are never printed. A failed upload remains failed until the service proves success. (7 October 2026)

